Born in the non-rotating zero-g keel of the generational ark Ogun-IV during Year 142 of its voyage to Proxima Centauri, Bulkhead Lilt was invented by third-generation maintenance crews and hydroponic agrarians trapped between dead ancestors and an unseen destination. Stripped of wood, soil, and open air, youth subcultures congregated in abandoned heat-exchange silos for unauthorized "purge-parties," swigging moonshine brewed from fermented nutrient algae and wearing utilitarian EVA suits decorated with hand-knotted copper wiring and woven raffia insulation. The music served as both a somatic rebellion against the ship's totalitarian mechanical hum and a physical exercise to prevent zero-gravity muscle atrophy: dancers locked arms in massive centrifugal rings, stomping against metal floor gratings to force the ship’s hull to sing in sympathetic resonance with the brass.

